/*
 * antd v6's `@rc-component/tabs` renamed the pane wrapper chain: the outer holder is
 * `ant-tabs-body-holder` (was `ant-tabs-content-holder`), the middle div is `ant-tabs-body`
 * (was `ant-tabs-content`), and each pane itself is `ant-tabs-content`/`ant-tabs-content-active`
 * (was `ant-tabs-tabpane`/`ant-tabs-tabpane-active`). ng-zorro now emits the new outer/middle
 * names next to the old ones (no v4 collision for `ant-tabs-body*`), but the per-pane element
 * keeps only the old `ant-tabs-tabpane`/`-active`/`-hidden` names: the compiled v4 CSS already
 * has a bare `.ant-tabs-content { position: relative; width: 100% }` rule for the *middle*
 * wrapper, so adding that same class name to the *pane* element would additionally apply that
 * rule to every pane under the v4 (default) stylesheet. The two v6 rules that key off the
 * per-pane `.ant-tabs-content` class are mirrored here onto the kept `.ant-tabs-tabpane` hook.
 */
.ant-tabs .ant-tabs-tabpane:focus-visible {
  outline: var(--ant-line-width-focus) solid var(--ant-color-primary-border);
  outline-offset: 1px;
  transition: outline-offset 0s, outline 0s;
}

/*
 * v6: `.ant-tabs-left > .ant-tabs-body-holder > .ant-tabs-body > .ant-tabs-content { padding-left }`
 * (and the right-side mirror). ng-zorro's pane element never carries `ant-tabs-content`, so the
 * rule is re-keyed on the chain of classes the pane actually has. Needed even though v4's own
 * `position.less` already has the equivalent rule on the old class chain, because the doc site's
 * `?theme=v6` mode loads only this v6 bundle and never loads the v4 stylesheet at all.
 */
.ant-tabs-left > .ant-tabs-content-holder > .ant-tabs-content > .ant-tabs-tabpane,
.ant-tabs-left > div > .ant-tabs-content-holder > .ant-tabs-content > .ant-tabs-tabpane {
  padding-left: var(--ant-padding-lg);
}
.ant-tabs-right > .ant-tabs-content-holder > .ant-tabs-content > .ant-tabs-tabpane,
.ant-tabs-right > div > .ant-tabs-content-holder > .ant-tabs-content > .ant-tabs-tabpane {
  padding-right: var(--ant-padding-lg);
}

/*
 * antd v6 tracks a JS-driven `ant-tabs-tab-focus` state class on the tab for its keyboard-focus
 * ring (`node_modules/@rc-component/tabs/es/TabNavList/TabNode.js`'s `focus` prop) and keys three
 * rules off `.ant-tabs-tab.ant-tabs-tab-focus .ant-tabs-tab-btn:focus-visible` (and the card-mode
 * variant with `:has()`). Since the tab in this DOM only ever receives `:focus-visible` through
 * its `.ant-tabs-tab-btn` child, `:has()` on that same child gives an identical result without
 * adding a bookkeeping class or new input.
 */
.ant-tabs .ant-tabs-tab .ant-tabs-tab-btn:focus-visible {
  outline: var(--ant-line-width-focus) solid var(--ant-color-primary-border);
  outline-offset: 1px;
  transition: outline-offset 0s, outline 0s;
}
.ant-tabs-card > .ant-tabs-nav .ant-tabs-tab:has(.ant-tabs-tab-btn:focus-visible),
.ant-tabs-card > div > .ant-tabs-nav .ant-tabs-tab:has(.ant-tabs-tab-btn:focus-visible) {
  outline: var(--ant-line-width-focus) solid var(--ant-color-primary-border);
  outline-offset: -3px;
  transition: outline-offset 0s, outline 0s;
}
.ant-tabs-card > .ant-tabs-nav .ant-tabs-tab:has(.ant-tabs-tab-btn:focus-visible) .ant-tabs-tab-btn:focus-visible,
.ant-tabs-card > div > .ant-tabs-nav .ant-tabs-tab:has(.ant-tabs-tab-btn:focus-visible) .ant-tabs-tab-btn:focus-visible {
  outline: none;
}

/*
 * The overflow ("more") menu reuses `nz-dropdown`/`ant-dropdown` (its pane already gets the
 * trigger's theme scope for free from dropdown's own wiring) instead of antd v6's bespoke
 * `ant-tabs-dropdown` element — the same structural choice v4's own `patch.less` already makes
 * (`.nz-tabs-dropdown.ant-dropdown .ant-dropdown-menu {...}`). `ant-tabs-dropdown` itself is not
 * emitted on the pane root: the compiled v4 CSS already has an absolute-positioned, z-indexed
 * `.ant-tabs-dropdown` rule from the pre-CDK era that would collapse the CDK overlay pane
 * (the same hazard the global constraints call out for CDK-positioned popup roots). v6's own
 * `.ant-tabs-dropdown .ant-tabs-dropdown-menu*` rules are mirrored onto the `nz-tabs-dropdown`
 * hook actually present in the DOM instead.
 */
.nz-tabs-dropdown.ant-dropdown {
  position: relative;
}
.nz-tabs-dropdown.ant-dropdown .ant-dropdown-menu {
  max-height: 200px;
  padding: var(--ant-padding-xxs) 0;
  overflow-x: hidden;
  overflow-y: auto;
  border-radius: var(--ant-border-radius-lg);
  box-shadow: var(--ant-box-shadow-secondary);
}
.nz-tabs-dropdown.ant-dropdown .ant-dropdown-menu-item.ant-tabs-dropdown-menu-item {
  min-width: 120px;
  padding: var(--ant-padding-xxs) var(--ant-padding-sm);
}
.nz-tabs-dropdown.ant-dropdown .ant-dropdown-menu-item.ant-tabs-dropdown-menu-item:hover {
  background: var(--ant-control-item-bg-hover);
}
.nz-tabs-dropdown.ant-dropdown .ant-dropdown-menu-item.ant-tabs-dropdown-menu-item-disabled,
.nz-tabs-dropdown.ant-dropdown .ant-dropdown-menu-item.ant-tabs-dropdown-menu-item-disabled:hover {
  color: var(--ant-color-text-disabled);
  background: transparent;
  cursor: not-allowed;
}
/*
 * ng-zorro's timeline item keeps its v4 element tree for the default stylesheet:
 *
 *   li.ant-timeline-item.ant-timeline-item-wrapper
 *     div.ant-timeline-item-label.ant-timeline-item-header.ant-timeline-item-title   (when labelled)
 *     div.ant-timeline-item-tail.ant-timeline-item-rail                              (not on the last node)
 *     div.ant-timeline-item-head.ant-timeline-item-icon
 *     div.ant-timeline-item-content
 *
 * antd v6 renders Timeline as a dot-type Steps: `li.-item > -item-wrapper > (-item-icon, -item-section >
 * (-item-header > (-item-title, -item-rail), -item-content))`, styled by Steps' rules copied onto the
 * `ant-timeline` prefix. The v4 stylesheet pairs `.ant-timeline-item-last > .ant-timeline-item-tail` and
 * `.ant-timeline-item-last > .ant-timeline-item-content`, so no wrapper or section element is inserted:
 * the item itself carries `-item-wrapper`, and the rules below give the content antd's section box and
 * the item antd's section layout in the alternate layout. The rail and the icon are absolutely
 * positioned against the item in both trees.
 */

/* antd draws no rail under the last node shown (`@rc-component/steps` Step.js: `!last && <Rail>`); the
   kept v4 tail would otherwise be an in-flow flex item adding a column gap before the icon. */
.ant-timeline .ant-timeline-item-tail:not(.ant-timeline-item-rail) {
  display: none;
}

/* antd `.ant-timeline-title-horizontal .ant-timeline-item-section`: the section takes the row beside the
   icon. The content is the section's only in-flow part here (antd's header holds only the rail and is
   empty without a title, and a labelled item always uses the alternate layout below). */
.ant-timeline-title-horizontal .ant-timeline-item-wrapper > .ant-timeline-item-content {
  flex: 1;
  min-width: 0;
}

/* antd `.ant-timeline:not(.ant-timeline-horizontal).ant-timeline-layout-alternate .ant-timeline-item
   .ant-timeline-item-section { display: flex; flex-wrap: nowrap; gap: … }`: the item is the flex row
   holding the header (label) and the content; the icon and the rail are absolute there. */
.ant-timeline:not(.ant-timeline-horizontal).ant-timeline-layout-alternate .ant-timeline-item-wrapper {
  column-gap: var(--ant-timeline-alternate-gap);
}

/* antd always renders `-item-header` (empty without a title), so the header's flex share keeps the
   content on its own side of the dot; ng-zorro renders the label only when set, so a pseudo-element
   takes the empty header's place. Values: antd's alternate `-item-header` flex basis and, for
   `-item-placement-end`, its `order: 1`. */
.ant-timeline:not(.ant-timeline-horizontal).ant-timeline-layout-alternate
  .ant-timeline-item-wrapper.ant-timeline-item-empty-header::before {
  content: '';
  flex: 1 1 calc(var(--ant-timeline-head-span-ptg) - var(--ant-timeline-alternate-gap) / 2);
}

.ant-timeline:not(.ant-timeline-horizontal).ant-timeline-layout-alternate
  .ant-timeline-item-wrapper.ant-timeline-item-empty-header.ant-timeline-item-placement-end::before {
  order: 1;
}

/* antd renders the rail before a loading/pending item solid: its `.ant-steps .ant-steps-item` rule resets
   the generated `dotted` process-rail style, and that rule never applies to ng-zorro items (they carry no
   `ant-steps-*` class). The doubled class outranks the generated `.ant-timeline .ant-timeline-item`. */
.ant-timeline .ant-timeline-item.ant-timeline-item-wrapper {
  --ant-cmp-steps-item-process-rail-line-style: solid;
}
/* CDK overlay pane positioning; v6's `position: absolute` would collapse the pane to 0x0 inside the
   CDK overlay pane (which is itself positioned), mispositioning the bubble. Port of `style/patch.less`. */
.ant-tooltip {
  position: relative;
}
/*
 * antd v6 marks a checked row with `.ant-transfer-list-content-item-checked` and a one-way target list
 * with `.ant-transfer-list-content-show-remove`. The v4 stylesheet styles both classes as well (a blue
 * checked row, a transparent hover in one-way lists), so ng-zorro does not emit them. The rules below are
 * antd's, keyed onto structure the rows already have instead:
 * - a checked row is one whose checkbox is checked (`label.ant-checkbox-wrapper > .ant-checkbox-checked`);
 *   the `:not(-disabled)` guard matches antd, which never marks a disabled row as checked;
 * - a one-way row is one with no checkbox (`nzOneWay` renders the item text and a remove button instead).
 */

/* antd: `.ant-transfer .ant-transfer-section .ant-transfer-list-content-item-checked` */
.ant-transfer
  .ant-transfer-section
  .ant-transfer-list-content-item:not(.ant-transfer-list-content-item-disabled):has(
    > .ant-checkbox-wrapper > .ant-checkbox-checked
  ) {
  background-color: var(--ant-control-item-bg-active);
}

/* antd: `… .ant-transfer-list-content-item:not(.ant-transfer-list-content-item-disabled).ant-transfer-list-content-item-checked:hover` */
.ant-transfer
  .ant-transfer-section
  .ant-transfer-list-content-item:not(.ant-transfer-list-content-item-disabled):has(
    > .ant-checkbox-wrapper > .ant-checkbox-checked
  ):hover {
